  {
    "episode_id": 74769,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74769.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "And with my McKinley Carter hat on for a minute, I mean, this is a big part of our work. While we are very successful, we manage over a billion dollars of liquid assets for our clients, both in the region and beyond. We have managed assets for clients in some 25 states. But the reality is the most significant assets and literally in virtually all of our client portfolios is their illiquid assets. It's their privately held businesses. It's their commercial real estate. It's a variety of non-traditional investments. And we spend an awful lot of time working with them, helping them think through how to manage the total financial situation, that portfolio of assets. How do they prepare generations? How do they prepare management internally for succession of responsibilities? If you would have asked me 20 years ago, early in my career, would this have been what we were doing? I don't think so. But we learn as we move along, what are the needs of our clients? And that clearly has become among them.",
