    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74769.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "For me, it's obvious. You know, I can be in Pittsburgh in under an hour, you know, international airport. I can have all the pleasures of a large city, but I get the benefits of a small city where you have real relationships with your family and friends. There's a level of authenticity that I have not observed in larger metropolitan areas. from a financial standpoint, I think about the low cost of living. We live in a very nice home at $125 a square foot. You can buy a lot of home and live a very comfortable lifestyle in West Virginia. What's interesting about that to go a step further is we are competing in a global marketplace. We work on the same V schedule that they do in downtown New York City. We have some significant advantages as it relates to efficiencies and profitability. And you might ask, well, can you compete? You're so far out of New York City or so far out of San Francisco or Chicago. I couldn't be more proud of how our team is stacked up against people all over the world and throughout the country. In fact, it was just a couple years ago, the last year the CNBC designated 100 best firms in the United States. This firm, based in Wheeling, West Virginia, was ranked 81st. You know, we compete. We're serving clients all over the country. And for that matter, in five or six other countries, while there's a predominance in the mid-Atlantic states, we do have clients in many other places. And we've been very proud of the discipline that we take and that authenticity we spoke of, genuine care for helping clients do in the right thing, don't cut corners, fulfill all of your obligations. And I think that there are many industries that could find themselves in the same situation, living and working in Appalachia.",
